title: Exactly two
mode: draw
chapter: Your own chips
check: behaves
parts: and or not
---- intro
The majority circuit lights when **at least** two switches are on. Now light it for **exactly** two: two on yes, three on no.

That is "at least two, AND NOT all three". You have built both halves already in this course: the majority circuit, and the chain of ANDs for all three.
---- task
Light `y` when exactly two of `a`, `b` and `c` are on.
---- start
input a 0 0
input b 0 80
input c 0 160
led y 640 80
---- reference
input a, b, c
led y
m = (a & b) | (a & c) | (b & c)
y = m & ~(a & b & c)
---- answer
input a 0 0
input b 0 80
input c 0 160
and g1 130 0
and g2 130 80
and g3 130 160
or o1 260 40
or o2 360 80
and t1 260 220
not n1 380 220
and y1 500 80
led y 640 80
a -> g1.0
b -> g1.1
a -> g2.0
c -> g2.1
b -> g3.0
c -> g3.1
g1 -> o1.0
g2 -> o1.1
o1 -> o2.0
g3 -> o2.1
g1 -> t1.0
c -> t1.1
t1 -> n1.in
o2 -> y1.0
n1 -> y1.1
y1 -> y
---- hint1
Build the majority first: three ANDs for the pairs, two ORs to join them. Then "all three" is a pair AND the third switch.
---- hint2
"All three" goes through a NOT, and one last AND joins it with the majority: at least two, and not three.
---- solution
Three ANDs and two ORs give "at least two". The `a AND b` gate ANDed with `c` gives "all three"; a NOT turns it round, and a last AND keeps the majority only when not all three are on.
---- done
This is the biggest circuit so far, and every piece of it was something you had built before. That is the whole trick: big circuits are small circuits you already know.
